Subject: engine: values are one 64-bit word, so a double's bits and a tag share it (QJS_NAN_BOXING64, off by default)

What this does
  With `QJS_NAN_BOXING64` on, `JSValue` is a `uint64_t`. The top 19 bits are the tag offset T and
  the low 45 are the payload P: a non-double with tag `t` is `(t - JS_TAG_FIRST) << 45 | payload`, a
  double is its IEEE bits plus `17 << 45`, and a pointer is the address shifted right by three. A
  double's T is therefore always at least `JS_TAG_FLOAT64`, so `JS_TAG_IS_FLOAT64` and
  `JS_VALUE_GET_NORM_TAG` keep the meaning they have in the 32-bit encoding. Every special value and
  every constructor is a compile-time constant expression.

  `JS_VALUE_IS_BOTH_INT` tests each operand's upper 32 bits against the int tag's T, because the
  16-byte form's `(tag1 | tag2) == 0` relies on `JS_TAG_INT` being zero. `JS_VALUE_HAS_REF_COUNT` is
  `T <= 8 && T != 5`, the second term being the lazy-JSON marker. `JS_MKPTR` asserts an 8-aligned
  pointer below `1 << 48` in a debug build.

  Every register-file stride, register-operand decode and light-frame offset now derives from
  `sizeof(JSValue)` or `offsetof` instead of a 16-byte assumption: the encoder's operand scale, the
  wire encode and decode, the monolith's `RD_REG`/`RD_REG8`, the profiler's register index, the
  accumulator-append call sites, the AOT iterator helpers, and the light prefix, which is now "the
  bytes before the record-only tail" rather than nine 16-byte slots.

  The register bytecode's narrow operand is written as a register index in wire units
  (`REGVM_WIRE_REG_SCALE`) rather than as a byte offset into the writer's register file, so the
  serialized stream does not depend on the writer's value size. The same source therefore writes the
  same bytes in either encoding, and the checked-in builtin bundles load and run in both without
  being rebuilt.

  A profile header carries `vmode=` and `QJS_AOT_ABI` carries the encoding in bit 9, because a
  profile and a compiled unit describe the build's layouts; the layout's `off_jsvalue_*` fields are
  zeroed under the flag, so the layout hash differs too.

  `JS_NewRuntime` checks that a heap block and a stack address fit the 48-bit encoding and refuses to
  start otherwise, printing the offending heap address. `JS_DupValueRT` and `JS_FreeValueRT` assert in
  a debug build that a counted tag never carries a NULL pointer.

  The 16-byte build is unchanged: same structure, same macro values, same bytecode header, same
  serialized bytes, same generated code.

Why we need it
  A 16-byte value doubles the register file, every property slot and every argument window, and the
  tag sits in a separate word from the payload, so a tag test and a payload load are two cache
  accesses. One word per value halves all of those and makes the tag a shift of the same word the
  payload comes from. The encoding is a flag so the 16-byte build stays the reference the AOT
  generator's output is checked against.

Hazards
  The encoding is not a macro rename: the compiler catches the `.u`/`.tag` accesses, and six classes
  of site that compile either way are silently wrong. Each is fixed here and each was observed, not
  reasoned about:

  1. An exact double-tag test. `JS_VALUE_GET_TAG(v) == JS_TAG_FLOAT64` is false for every double,
     because a double's tag is `57` before normalisation. Fifty-one sites compared tags exactly, in
     the relational and equality slow paths, the register VM's fused arithmetic, fused compare and
     deopt fast paths, the packed-array kind helpers, the AOT runtime's helpers and the generator's
     constant-pool scan; all now use `JS_TAG_IS_FLOAT64`. Before the fix `-0 === 0` was false inside
     a function, `0 * -5` lost its sign, and four corpus cases differed.

  2. `sizeof(JSValue)` used as another struct's stride. `JSRegPIC.slot_off` was
     `slot * sizeof(JSValue)`, which coincided with `JSProperty`'s size only while a value was 16
     bytes; every cached property read then returned a slot at the wrong offset. It is now
     `slot * sizeof(JSProperty)`, and the reverse division in the add-transition replay matches.

  3. A hard-coded 16. `REGVM_LIGHT_PREFIX_BYTES` was nine 16-byte slots. The nine is a count of
     fields, which pack two-to-a-slot only while a value is 16 bytes; the prefix must end where the
     record-only tail begins, so it is `offsetof(struct JSRegRun, ctx)` and the slot count is derived.

  4. All-bits-zero used as a value. Zero is `T = 0`, the big-int tag, with a NULL pointer: a register
     filled that way faults on its first release or mark. The register-file fills, including the
     never-written-register fill and the AOT gate mutant, now use `regvm_fill_unwritten`, which
     writes integer 0 in both encodings; the debug assertion in the value free and dup paths catches
     any remaining one at the point it is first touched.

  5. A two-word copy or compare, and a file whose bytes depend on the writer's layouts. No `memcmp`
     or `memcpy` treats a value as 16 bytes: the copies are `sizeof(JSValue)`-sized and the 16-byte SSE
     store is compiled out. The one place a layout did leak into a file was the narrow register
     operand, which stored a byte offset into the writer's register file; it now stores a register
     index, so the same source writes the same bytecode in either encoding. Everything else a file
     holds is a tag, a payload or a register index, all of which are size-independent.

  6. The generated code's prelude. What this patch owes that class is only the engine's half: the
     layout export prints no tag or union offset for a one-word value, and the ABI and the layout
     hash carry the encoding so a unit built against the other one refuses to bind.

  NaN payloads do not survive a value: `__JS_NewFloat64` canonicalises every NaN to one quiet NaN and
  asserts it, so a payload written through a DataView into a Float64Array reads back as a NaN that
  neither compares, hashes nor serialises by its payload.

  Pointer range: the encoding keeps 45 bits of an address, which covers the 48-bit user space. A
  build on a target that tags heap pointers in their top byte (Android 11 and later) must set
  `android:allowNativeHeapPointerTagging="false"`; iOS on arm64 needs nothing, since user addresses
  fit and pointer authentication covers code pointers rather than data. The runtime check makes a
  target that does not fit fail at startup instead of corrupting addresses.

  A profile and a compiled unit from the other encoding are refused, through the ABI bit and the
  layout hash, because those do encode the build's layouts; a bytecode file is not refused, because
  it does not. Compiling and linking two translation units with different flags is undefined; the ABI
  is not compatible and this is documented beside the flag.

